{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"n1","type":"text","text":"### ⚙️ SQLAlchemy Core Components\n\n• [[Engine]] establishes connection to database.\n• [[Declarative Base]] maps Python classes to database tables.\n• [[Session]] manages interactions and transactions.\n• [[sessionmaker]] is a factory for Session instances.\n\n---\n[[Gemini/SQLAlchemy ORM 核心概念梳理/007-我要重新梳理下_sqlalchemy_的几个点.md|QA7]] [[Gemini/SQLAlchemy ORM 核心概念梳理/011-sqlalchemy_我又忘记了.md|QA11]]","x":270,"y":100,"width":360,"height":406,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"n2","type":"text","text":"### 🛠️ Engine & Metadata Role\n\n• `create_engine()` configures database connection details, creating a [[Connection Pool]].\n• `Base.metadata` represents the collection of all defined table schemas (the 'blueprints').\n• `Base.metadata.create_all(engine)` uses the Engine to create tables in the database based on the metadata.\n\n---\n[[Gemini/SQLAlchemy ORM 核心概念梳理/007-我要重新梳理下_sqlalchemy_的几个点.md|QA7]] [[Gemini/SQLAlchemy ORM 核心概念梳理/011-sqlalchemy_我又忘记了.md|QA11]]","x":60,"y":626,"width":360,"height":432,"color":"0"},
		{"id":"n3","type":"text","text":"### 🔄 Session Management Lifecycle\n\n• `sessionmaker(bind=engine)` configures the Session class to bind to a specific [[Engine]]. This is a one-time setup.\n• A `session` instance (`SessionLocal()`) acts as a 'staging area' for database operations (add, delete, update).\n• Session instances are 'lazy'; they only establish a physical [[Database Connection]] when the first operation is performed.\n• Operations like `flush()` and `commit()` manage data persistence and transaction boundaries.\n\n---\n[[Gemini/SQLAlchemy ORM 核心概念梳理/008-可以这么总结吗：Session_=_sessionmakerbind=engin.md|QA8]] [[Gemini/SQLAlchemy ORM 核心概念梳理/009-所以这个类的实例就是连接数据库的实际的会话.md|QA9]] [[Gemini/SQLAlchemy ORM 核心概念梳理/011-sqlalchemy_我又忘记了.md|QA11]]","x":480,"y":626,"width":360,"height":562,"color":"0"},
		{"id":"group-p1","type":"group","label":"SQLAlchemy ORM Fundamentals","x":0,"y":0,"width":900,"height":1248,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"n4","type":"text","text":"### 🔗 SQLAlchemy Relationships & Back-Populates\n\n• `relationship()` defines object-level navigation between related models in Python.\n• `back_populates` explicitly links two `relationship` attributes as mutual mirrors.\n• This contrasts with [[ForeignKey]] which is a database-level physical link.\n\n---\n[[Gemini/SQLAlchemy ORM 核心概念梳理/001-哎呀_求求你了_你太爱用比喻了_我不需要_我只要搞清楚关系.md|QA1]] [[Gemini/SQLAlchemy ORM 核心概念梳理/012-我不太明白_relationship_和_backpopulates.md|QA12]] [[Gemini/SQLAlchemy ORM 核心概念梳理/014-我觉得好搞啊.md|QA14]] [[Gemini/SQLAlchemy ORM 核心概念梳理/015-我觉得我就是对_backpopulates_这个怪怪的名字不理解.md|QA15]]","x":1630,"y":100,"width":360,"height":432,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"n5","type":"text","text":"### ↔️ ForeignKey vs. Relationship\n\n• [[ForeignKey]] is a column in the database table, establishing a physical link (e.g., `customer_id` in `Order` table).\n• [[relationship]] is a Python attribute (not a database column) that provides 'smart navigation' between objects (e.g., `order.customer` or `customer.orders`).\n• `ForeignKey` provides a raw ID; `relationship` provides the linked object(s).\n\n---\n[[Gemini/SQLAlchemy ORM 核心概念梳理/012-我不太明白_relationship_和_backpopulates.md|QA12]]","x":1210,"y":652,"width":360,"height":510,"color":"0"},
		{"id":"n6","type":"text","text":"### 🔁 Understanding back_populates\n\n• `back_populates` tells SQLAlchemy that two `relationship` attributes in different classes refer to each other.\n• It ensures consistency and allows [[bidirectional navigation]] between objects.\n• The value of `back_populates` must strictly match the attribute name in the *other* class.\n• It 'fills in' the reverse side of a relationship, which isn't automatically implied by a single `ForeignKey`.\n\n---\n[[Gemini/SQLAlchemy ORM 核心概念梳理/001-哎呀_求求你了_你太爱用比喻了_我不需要_我只要搞清楚关系.md|QA1]] [[Gemini/SQLAlchemy ORM 核心概念梳理/015-我觉得我就是对_backpopulates_这个怪怪的名字不理解.md|QA15]]","x":1630,"y":652,"width":360,"height":510,"color":"0"},
		{"id":"n7","type":"text","text":"### 🧭 Relationship Navigation Types\n\n• `relationship` can return a single object (e.g., `order.customer` returns one `Customer` object).\n• `relationship` can return a list/collection of objects (e.g., `customer.orders` returns a list of `Order` objects).\n• The type of return (single vs. collection) depends on the [[One-to-Many]] or [[Many-to-One]] nature of the relationship.\n\n---\n[[Gemini/SQLAlchemy ORM 核心概念梳理/014-我觉得好搞啊.md|QA14]]","x":2050,"y":652,"width":360,"height":484,"color":"0"},
		{"id":"n8","type":"text","text":"### ✨ Django ORM Relationship Simplicity\n\n• Django automatically infers and creates bidirectional relationships (forward and reverse access) simply by defining a [[ForeignKey]].\n• This is a key difference from SQLAlchemy, where `relationship` and `back_populates` are explicitly required for object-level navigation.\n• Django's approach simplifies [[ORM]] usage for common relationship patterns.\n\n---\n[[Gemini/SQLAlchemy ORM 核心概念梳理/013-那django是天然支持_Foreign_Key_在对象层面上去搜索关联表别的属.md|QA13]]","x":1630,"y":1282,"width":360,"height":484,"color":"0"},
		{"id":"group-p2","type":"group","label":"SQLAlchemy ORM Relationships & Pitfalls","x":1150,"y":0,"width":1320,"height":1826,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"n9","type":"text","text":"### 🤖 LangChain Structured Output\n\n• `with_structured_output()` enables LLMs to return data directly as [[Pydantic]] objects, bypassing manual JSON parsing.\n• This leverages [[Function Calling]] capabilities of LLMs.\n• Placeholders like `{today}` in prompts are 'holes' to be filled dynamically at runtime.\n\n---\n[[Gemini/SQLAlchemy ORM 核心概念梳理/002-这段怎么这么奇怪，没有让用户提供today，但是又在花括号里用了_{today}.md|QA2]] [[Gemini/SQLAlchemy ORM 核心概念梳理/003-structuredllm_=_llm.md|QA3]]","x":270,"y":2176,"width":360,"height":432,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"n10","type":"text","text":"### 📝 Dynamic Prompt Placeholders\n\n• Curly braces `{variable_name}` in prompts (e.g., in LangChain or f-strings) define placeholders.\n• These are not keywords but 'slots' for values to be injected later during prompt formatting.\n• This allows for [[Dynamic Prompt Generation]] based on runtime data.\n\n---\n[[Gemini/SQLAlchemy ORM 核心概念梳理/002-这段怎么这么奇怪，没有让用户提供today，但是又在花括号里用了_{today}.md|QA2]]","x":60,"y":2728,"width":360,"height":406,"color":"0"},
		{"id":"n11","type":"text","text":"### ↔️ Pydantic Object to Prompt Input\n\n• LLM structured outputs (e.g., `OrderCheck` instance) are [[Pydantic Objects]].\n• When passing Pydantic objects to a [[PromptTemplate]], convert them to a [[Python Dictionary]] using `.model_dump()` (Pydantic v2) or `.dict()` (Pydantic v1).\n• This ensures proper formatting for the LLM to interpret the structured data.\n\n---\n[[Gemini/SQLAlchemy ORM 核心概念梳理/004-那我想把这个result_作为_information传入这个chain.md|QA4]]","x":480,"y":2728,"width":360,"height":484,"color":"0"},
		{"id":"group-p3","type":"group","label":"LLM Structured Output & Data Handling","x":0,"y":2076,"width":900,"height":1196,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"n12","type":"text","text":"### 💻 Dev Environment & Agent Control Flow\n\n• Common issues with [[VS Code Jupyter Notebook]] include kernel freezing and UI unresponsiveness.\n• Solutions involve reloading the window, switching kernels, or using the [[Interactive Window]] with `.py` files.\n• In [[LangGraph]], `Handoff` signifies the transfer of control flow between [[Multi-Agent Systems]].\n\n---\n[[Gemini/SQLAlchemy ORM 核心概念梳理/005-vscode_jupyter_notebook_有时候会一直运行不成功，我res.md|QA5]] [[Gemini/SQLAlchemy ORM 核心概念梳理/010-okk_jupyter_notebook我快忍不了了_动不动就卡死.md|QA10]] [[Gemini/SQLAlchemy ORM 核心概念梳理/006-handoff_from_supervisor_to_agent_这里.md|QA6]]","x":1630,"y":2176,"width":360,"height":458,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"n13","type":"text","text":"### ⚠️ VS Code Jupyter Troubleshooting\n\n• Frequent freezing in [[VS Code Jupyter Notebook]] is often due to a deadlocked Python kernel or UI state issues.\n• Recommended solutions: `Reload Window` (Ctrl+Shift+P), attempting to switch/restart kernel.\n• For persistent issues, consider using the [[Interactive Window]] with `.py` files instead of `.ipynb`.\n\n---\n[[Gemini/SQLAlchemy ORM 核心概念梳理/005-vscode_jupyter_notebook_有时候会一直运行不成功，我res.md|QA5]] [[Gemini/SQLAlchemy ORM 核心概念梳理/010-okk_jupyter_notebook我快忍不了了_动不动就卡死.md|QA10]]","x":1210,"y":2754,"width":360,"height":458,"color":"0"},
		{"id":"n14","type":"text","text":"### 🚀 Interactive Window for Python Development\n\n• The [[VS Code Interactive Window]] allows running code cells (`# %%`) within a standard `.py` file.\n• This offers a more stable alternative to `.ipynb` notebooks, reducing freezing and performance issues.\n• It provides the same interactive execution experience without the overhead of the `.ipynb` file format.\n\n---\n[[Gemini/SQLAlchemy ORM 核心概念梳理/010-okk_jupyter_notebook我快忍不了了_动不动就卡死.md|QA10]]","x":1630,"y":2754,"width":360,"height":432,"color":"0"},
		{"id":"n15","type":"text","text":"### 🤝 Agent Handoff in LangGraph\n\n• `Handoff` in [[LangGraph]] refers to the transfer of execution control and context between different [[Agents]].\n• A [[Supervisor Agent]] typically decides which [[Worker Agent]] should take over a task.\n• This mechanism is crucial for building complex [[Multi-Agent Systems]] where different agents specialize in different tasks.\n\n---\n[[Gemini/SQLAlchemy ORM 核心概念梳理/006-handoff_from_supervisor_to_agent_这里.md|QA6]]","x":2050,"y":2754,"width":360,"height":484,"color":"0"},
		{"id":"group-p4","type":"group","label":"Development Environment & Agent Orchestration","x":1150,"y":2076,"width":1320,"height":1222,"color":"4"}
	],
